# Pulling scRNA-seq Datasets in R
The datasets used in the paper all can be pulled using the R library scRNAseq. This library belongs to Bioconductor,
an open-source software project. This project hosts many libraries with different tools for analyzing scRNA-seq data.
The installation code for the Bioconductor libraries used in the notebook are found in:
https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.19/bioc/
The R Notebook file DataLoading.rmd shows an example of loading one scRNA-seq dataset.
The full list of scRNA-seq datasets stored in Bioconductor as well as the R code to load them in R 
can be found in: https://bioconductor.org/packages/devel/data/experiment/manuals/scRNAseq/man/scRNAseq.pdf
For our study we employ simple and standard processing Bioconductor functions to the datasets. 
For details on the purpose and mechanism of these commands see: Orchestrating Single-Cell Analysis with 
Bioconductor by Amezquita et al (https://bioconductor.org/books/3.12/OSCA/). 

# Formatting Data for Matlab Loading
After applying PCA to the processed dataset we compute or determine on the reduced space: 
- covariance matrix of each cell cluster per sample. 
- mean vector of each cell cluster per sample on the reduced space. 
- cluster proportions relative to the samples they belong to. Saved as *ww*.
- the number of cells per sample. Saved as *numpersample*
- the number of clusters per sample. Saved as *stride*
- the ground truth label of each cluster. Saved as *cellnames*
The first two objects are stored together as a single matrix called *supp*: The mean vector of each cluster is stacked vertically
with its vectorized covariance matrix. The resulting columns, correspoding to all clusters across all samples, 
are then horizontally concatenated into *supp*. As an example, if the number of principal components for a scRNA-seq dataset is 2
then the number of rows of its *supp* matrix is 6, while 50 principal components would imply 2550 rows for *supp*. The number of 
columns is equal to the total number of clusters in all samples. Below is a description of the other objects.
- *ww*: vector storing cluster proportions. Same length as *supp*.
- *numpersample*: vector storing the number of cells per sample. Its length is equal to the number of samples.
- *stride*: vector storing the number of clusters per sample. It has the same length as numpersample.
- *cellnames*: string vector storing the groundtruth labels of each cluster across all samples. Same length as *supp*.
These objects are stored in a .mat object which can easily be loaded in Matlab.
Two examples of formatted datasets are:
- baronpc: Baron Pancreas dataset. It has 4 real samples.
- segerSL: This dataset includes 20 simulated samples (10 for each class of patient healthy vs type 2 diabetes).
The R Notebook file DataLoading.rmd shows the code and detail for the described procedure


